\subsection{Router Training as Diagnostic Probe}
\label{sec:router-diagnostic}

We probe whether counterfactual labels can train a step-level router as a diagnostic of label informativeness. The oracle margin (2.89 points) caps routing upside; at step level, of 2{,}040 training steps only 0.3\% exhibit strict directional conflict (step-level oracle opposite to episode label) and 60.3\% are pre-event neutral (neither strategy advantaged)---a granularity mismatch, not label error---leaving minimal signal for the router. An implicit router (Qwen3, LoRA; Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}) trained at three scales (0.6B, 1.7B, 4B) with 4-seed configurations shows flat step-level balanced accuracy ($56$--$59\%$, within seed variance). At episode level, only 4B exceeds always-deterministic ($59.0 \pm 1.9\%$, $p < 0.005$; Appendix~\ref{app:ablations}); 0.6B and 1.7B remain at or below chance. The multi-step nature of routing (10--50 actions with compounding state uncertainty) creates inherent label noise between step-level decisions and episode-level outcomes. Training and validation use episode-level splits (Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}), ensuring no within-episode information leakage.

\paragraph{End-to-end deployment.} The 4B router deployed end-to-end ($N{=}245$) scores $-12.03$ (95\% CI: $[-17.82, -6.45]$), not exceeding always-deterministic ($-9.47$). Paraphrase-based augmentation ($25\%$--$800\%$) provides no improvement; the $3.7\%$ internal-preferred base rate makes real-data collection expensive (Appendices~\ref{app:seed-robustness}--\ref{app:ablations}).

tool_args.new_string:
\subsection{Router Training: A Negative Result}
\label{sec:router-diagnostic}

We probe whether counterfactual labels can train a step-level router, as a diagnostic of label informativeness. The oracle margin (2.89 points) caps routing upside. The near-chance step-level accuracy admits two interpretations: (A)~episode-level labels projected onto steps create ${\sim}$88\% uninformative signal (60.3\% pre-event neutral, 27.4\% post-event), drowning learnable patterns in noise; (B)~routing signal is genuinely task-type-level---there is no step-level pattern to learn. Evidence favors a mixture: directional conflict is only 0.3\% of 2{,}040 training steps (labels are directionally correct in 99.7\% of informative steps), but the granularity mismatch leaves minimal signal for the router.

An implicit router (Qwen3, LoRA; Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}) trained at three scales (0.6B, 1.7B, 4B) shows flat step-level balanced accuracy ($56$--$59\%$). At episode level, only 4B exceeds always-deterministic ($59.0 \pm 1.9\%$, $p < 0.005$); 0.6B and 1.7B remain at chance. Episode-level independent evaluation yields $47.1 \pm 2.2\%$, below the majority-vote baseline of 50.0\%. Training uses episode-level splits (Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}), ensuring no within-episode leakage. The high uninformative rate connects to noisy-label learning~\citep{natarajan2013learning}: standard ERM on labels with ${\sim}$88\% noise yields near-chance classifiers, consistent with our results.

\paragraph{End-to-end deployment.} The 4B router deployed end-to-end ($N{=}245$) scores $-12.03$ (95\% CI: $[-17.82, -6.45]$), not exceeding always-deterministic ($-9.47$). Death-proximal labeling (training only on steps within $k$ steps of death events) is a promising direction for extracting step-level signal from episode-level outcomes. Paraphrase-based augmentation ($25\%$--$800\%$) provides no improvement; the $3.7\%$ internal-preferred base rate makes real-data collection expensive (Appendices~\ref{app:seed-robustness}--\ref{app:ablations}).

